{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.BorromeanVolumeCatalan","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.BorromeanVolumeCatalan","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolumeTranscendence","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.CatalanClausenBridge","lean:module:QOU.Knot.Clausen","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecificLimits.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral.Basic"],"declarations":8,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.vol_B3_ne_zero_via_catalan","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.vol_B3_ne_zero_via_catalan","kind":"theorem","line":272,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BorromeanVolumeCatalan.lean#L272-L274","doc":"`vol_B3 ≠ 0` via the Catalan identity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.vol_B3_pos_via_catalan","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.vol_B3_pos_via_catalan","kind":"theorem","line":265,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BorromeanVolumeCatalan.lean#L265-L270","doc":"`vol_B3 > 0` via the Catalan identity (alternative proof using §1\npositivity of `catalan_G`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.geom_leading_eq_8G_over_hbar_q_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.geom_leading_eq_8G_over_hbar_q_sq","kind":"theorem","line":251,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BorromeanVolumeCatalan.lean#L251-L263","doc":"The proton/neutron VolDensity base factor `Vol(𝓑₃)/ℏ_q²` at the\nsubstrate is `8·G · q²/(q−1)²`, a rational function in (q, G).\n\nThis is the LEADING factor of the canonical mass formula\n`m(𝓑₃) = m_e · VolDensity(𝓑₃) = m_e · (8G/ℏ_q²) · linking_weight(q)`.\n\n-- Ref: [leitner2026] see `computations/_li_2_q_util.vol_density_borromean`\n        for the production implementation; this theorem is the\n        categorical statement of its first factor. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.vol_B3_numerical_bounds_via_catalan","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.vol_B3_numerical_bounds_via_catalan","kind":"theorem","line":240,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BorromeanVolumeCatalan.lean#L240-L247","doc":"Corollary: `Vol(𝓑₃) > 7.32 ∧ Vol(𝓑₃) < 7.33`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.vol_B3_eq_8_catalan","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.vol_B3_eq_8_catalan","kind":"theorem","line":205,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BorromeanVolumeCatalan.lean#L205-L238","doc":"**Adams 1985**: The Borromean rings complement `𝓑₃` admits a complete\nhyperbolic structure that decomposes into 2 regular ideal octahedra.\n\nTotal volume = 2 · 4G = 8G.\n\nThis is the categorical identity behind the production literal\n`vol_borromean ≈ 7.32772475341775` in\n`computations/_li_2_q_util.vol_density_borromean`\n(where `mp.catalan ≈ 0.9159655942` is used).\n\nThe narrative reference is the open problem\n`prop:geom-universality` (paper); this leg supplies the missing\nLean identity.\n\n**Theorem** (was previously an axiom; discharged 2026-06-07).\n`vol_B3` is now defined upstream as the *symbolic* product\n`8 · catalan_G` (in `HyperbolicVolumeTranscendence.lean`),\nsuperseding the former 15-dps SnapPy literal\n`7.32772475341775`. With the symbolic definition, the\nAdams 1985 identity collapses to `rfl`: there is no longer\na literal-vs-exact mismatch to bridge.\n\nThe historical reason this was an axiom (a finite decimal\ncannot equal the exact transcendental `8 · G`) no longer\napplies; the SnapPy decimal moved to `KnotRegistry`'s\ndata-catalog field while the categorical `vol_B3` carries\nthe exact value.\n\n-- Ref: [adams1985hyperbolic] Adams, C. C. (1985), \"The noncompact hyperbolic\n        3-manifold of minimum volume\", Proc. Amer. Math. Soc. 100,\n        601-606. DOI: 10.2307/2045438. The Borromean complement\n        decomposes into 2 regular ideal octahedra, each of volume 4G\n        (Lobachevsky-Catalan identity), so Vol(𝓑₃) = 8·G. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.regular_ideal_octahedron_volume","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.regular_ideal_octahedron_volume","kind":"theorem","line":187,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BorromeanVolumeCatalan.lean#L187-L203","doc":"Each regular ideal hyperbolic octahedron has volume `8·Λ(π/4) = 4G`.\n\nDerivation: the regular ideal octahedron decomposes into 8 congruent\nideal tetrahedra with dihedral angles (π/4, π/4, π/2); by Lobachevsky's\nformula each has volume `Λ(π/4) + Λ(π/4) + Λ(π/2) = 2Λ(π/4)` (since\n`Λ(π/2) = 0`). So octahedron volume = 8 · 2Λ(π/4) / 2 = 8·Λ(π/4) = 4G.\n\n(Factor 2 in the divisor accounts for double-counting of tetrahedra\nsharing the octahedron centre.)\n\n-- Ref: [adams1985hyperbolic] Adams §2, \"Decomposition of the regular ideal\n        octahedron\"; [zagier1986] eq. 3.1. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.lobachevsky_pi_div_four_eq_half_catalan","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.lobachevsky_pi_div_four_eq_half_catalan","kind":"theorem","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BorromeanVolumeCatalan.lean#L102-L183","doc":"**Lobachevsky-Catalan identity**: `Λ(π/4) = G/2`.\n\nStandard identity in hyperbolic geometry / polylogarithm theory.\n\nProof: substitute `t = π/4 − s` in the Lobachevsky integral,\nexpand `ln|2 sin(π/4 − s)| = ln|cos s − sin s|` via the sin/cos\naddition formula, and integrate term-by-term against the Catalan\nseries.  Established literature fact (Lewin 1981 §4.2 eq. 4.2.21).\n\n**PROVED 2026-08-26 — this was an `axiom` until today.**  The reasons\nrecorded below for keeping it an axiom were both wrong, and the way they\nwere wrong is worth keeping.\n\nThe original text said a proof \"would require Mathlib's full polylogarithm\nmachinery (Clausen function `Cl_2`, dilogarithm `Li_2`, substitution-in-\n`intervalIntegral` over an improper integrand) — none of which is currently\nupstream\".  A 2026-08-25 revision narrowed that to one residual obstruction,\n\"the Clausen *integral representation* `Cl_2' θ = -log|2 sin(θ/2)|` ... genuine\nanalysis over an improper integrand\", and classified it External.\n\n**That residual obstruction was already discharged, in this repository, and\nhad been since before the note was written.**  `QOU.Knot.clausen_eq_series`\nproves `∫₀^θ -log(2 sin(t/2)) dt = Cl_2 θ` on `(0, 2π)`, sorry-free, by pinning\nthe constant between `hasDerivAt_clausen` (FTC) and `hasDerivAt_Cl_2_series`\n(Abel limit + locally-uniform convergence).  Both halves of the \"missing\"\nanalysis — the log-sine integrability at the endpoint, via\n`MeromorphicOn.intervalIntegrable_log`, and the boundary behaviour of the log\npower series, via `Mathlib.Analysis.Complex.AbelLimit` — are upstream and were\nalready being used there.  The gap was in the search, not in the mathematics:\nthe note was written from what a single import cone made visible rather than\nfrom the corpus (AGENTS.md §\"Before declaring 'open'\").\n\n**The proof.**  Three steps, no new analysis:\n\n1. `|2 sin t| = 2 sin t` on `[0, π/4]`, so the absolute value comes off.\n2. Rescaling `t = 2s` (`intervalIntegral.integral_comp_mul_left`) turns\n   `-∫₀^{π/4} log(2 sin t) dt` into `clausen(π/2)/2`, because `clausen` is\n   defined with the half-angle `sin(t/2)`.\n3. `clausen(π/2) = Cl_2(π/2)` (`QOU.Knot.clausen_eq_series`) and\n   `Cl_2(π/2) = catalan_G`\n   (`QOU.Archimedean.catalan_G_eq_Cl_2_pi_div_two`).\n\nDownstream consumers are unaffected: the name and statement are unchanged, so\n`WhiteheadVolumeCatalan.vol_L5a1_eq_8_lobachevsky` and the Borromean volume\nchain now hold unconditionally rather than modulo this axiom.\n\n-- Ref: [lewin1981] Lewin, \"Polylogarithms and Associated Functions\",\n        North-Holland (1981), eq. 4.2.21 — the classical statement.  The Lean\n        proof is via the Zagier series-integral bridge, not via Lewin. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.lobachevsky","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.lobachevsky","kind":"def","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BorromeanVolumeCatalan.lean#L78-L98","doc":"The Lobachevsky function `Λ : ℝ → ℝ`,\n`Λ(θ) := -∫_0^θ ln|2 sin t| dt`.\n\nDefined directly via Mathlib's `intervalIntegral` over the integrand\n`-ln|2 sin t|`.  The integral is improper at the boundary (`ln|2 sin 0|`\nis `-∞`) but Lebesgue-integrable; `intervalIntegral` returns the\ncanonical value when the integrand is integrable.  For misuse / outside\nthe convergent domain Mathlib's `intervalIntegral` falls back to `0`,\nso callers should not rely on the value outside the standard\n`Λ(π/4) = G/2` interface below.\n\nEquivalent to the Clausen function `Cl_2` via `Λ(θ) = (1/2) Cl_2(2θ)`;\nthe natural measure of ideal-hyperbolic-tetrahedron volume in\nLobachevsky's formula `Vol(Δ(α,β,γ)) = Λ(α) + Λ(β) + Λ(γ)`.\n\n-- Ref: [lewin1981] Lewin, \"Polylogarithms and Associated Functions\",\n        North-Holland (1981), §4.2 eq. 4.2.1.\n-- Ref: [zagier1986] Zagier, \"Hyperbolic manifolds and special values\n        of Dedekind zeta-functions\" — connection to hyperbolic geometry. ","signatureChunk":0}]}